We will start with **direct substitution** (step A):

$\begin{align}
h\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)&=\dfrac{2\sin\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)}{1-\cos\left(2\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\right)\right)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}{1-0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{1}
\\\\
&=\sqrt{2}
\end{align}$

We got a number, so we've **found the limit** (step C)!

This means $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to \Large{\frac{\pi}{4}}}h(x)=\sqrt{2}$.
